Yes, I looked at it. It is Episode 4, “B+Tree node binary representation.” It explains representing node type, root flags, keys, and pointers as binary data so nodes can eventually be stored on disk. YouTube video (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bRjAglcPb_c)

You should not replace your current object-based B+ tree yet.

Your current implementation is useful for learning and testing the tree algorithms:

BPlusTreeNode
├── keys
├── LeafNode
└── InternalNode

Object representation is easier to debug and is appropriate for:

- searching;
- inserting;
- splitting leaves;
- splitting internal nodes;
- root replacement;
- range scans;
- testing tree invariants.

Byte representation becomes necessary when you want persistence. A database cannot safely save Java object references such as:

LeafNode nextLeaf;
List<Integer> keys;

Those references only make sense inside the current JVM. Instead, a persisted node needs data such as:

node type
number of keys
keys
record IDs or child offsets
next-leaf offset

The best architecture for your project is hybrid:

Disk:
4096 bytes
    ↓ deserialize
Java object node
    ↓ modify
Java object node
    ↓ serialize
4096 bytes on disk

So your eventual design should have classes similar to:

LeafNode              // in-memory logical representation
InternalNode          // in-memory logical representation
NodeSerializer        // converts objects ↔ ByteBuffer
IndexStorageManager   // reads/writes serialized nodes

Do not put ByteBuffer logic directly into every B+ tree algorithm. Keep the tree algorithms working with LeafNode and InternalNode, then add serialization around them.

One important note: your current code is not yet a persistent B+ tree. It still has delete() as a TODO, and nodes contain Java references rather than disk offsets. That is fine for the current in-memory phase.

Recommended next step:

1. Finish and test the in-memory B+ tree.
2. Define a fixed 4096-byte node format.
3. Implement NodeSerializer.
4. Test serialize → deserialize equality.
5. Only then connect nodes to an index file.

So the answer is: byte representation is better for the final disk-backed database, but object representation is better for your current algorithm-development stage. Do not rewrite everything; add serialization as the next layer.



